  "map_id": "map:amundsen-scott-south-pole-station",
  "method": "Plan positions of the 78 standard in-ice strings. Each string position is the mean horizontal (x, y) of its 60 DOMs in the IC86 geometry file published in the IceCube repository icecube/toise (strings are vertical; string 21 varies by 0.33 m, every other string is identical in all 60 DOMs). Frame and anchor: the IceCube coordinate system has its y-axis along the Prime Meridian (Grid North, towards Greenwich) and its x-axis 90 degrees clockwise from it (Grid East) (IceCube instrumentation paper, arXiv v3, section 5.3), and its origin is at elevation 883.9 m. The IceCube Collaboration software repository i3astropy gives the nominal location of the centre of the detector as lat -89.9944, lon -62.6081, height 883.9 m, which is taken as the frame origin. Conversion: the origin is placed at that point in a South Pole azimuthal-equidistant plane (distance from the pole = colatitude x 6 371 008.8 m, grid north = 0 deg meridian, grid east = 90 deg E; this is the same plane the browser draws), each string is the origin plus its (x, y) offset, and the inverse projection gives lat/lon. The origin is 622.7 m from the pole on the 62.6 deg W meridian. Each string is drawn as a 2 m x 2 m square aligned to grid north, a position marker only: the borehole is far smaller, so the size is symbolic and the 1 m half-width is not a claim about the hole. Accuracy: the layout of the strings relative to each other is surveyed to about 1 m, and the pattern keeps its true size and orientation (grid north). The absolute position of the whole pattern is only good to about 500 m: the anchor is a nominal value of no stated epoch, the array moves with the ice about 10 m a year relative to geographic coordinates, and the lat/lon of IceCube on Wikipedia and in OpenStreetMap (89.99 S, 63.4531 W) lies 489 m from the anchor used. Check done in the worklog: median nearest-neighbour spacing of strings 1-78 is 124.3 m against the official triangular grid of 125 m horizontal spacing.",
  "name": "IceCube standard in-ice strings 1-78 (plan positions)",
  "notes": [
    "The 78 strings sit on a triangular grid with 125 m horizontal spacing (nearest-neighbour median 124.3 m measured; the few shorter spacings are the irregular strings near DeepCore).",
    "DeepCore strings 79-86 are in geometry:icecube-deepcore-strings-79-86; the seven central standard strings that also belong to DeepCore (26, 27, 35, 36, 37, 45, 46) are drawn here only.",
    "Quote for the frame (IceCube instrumentation paper v3, section 5.3): \"The y-axis of the IceCube coordinate system is aligned with the Prime Meridian (Grid North), pointing toward Greenwich, UK.\" (the y is italic in the PDF) and \"The IceCube coordinate system origin is located at 46500’E, 52200’N, at an elevation of 2900 ft (883.9 m).\""
  ],
  "representation": "derived",
